ier Province, Sind and Baluchistan amalgamated in to a single State. Self-government within the British Empire or without the British Empire, the formation of a consolidated North-West Indian Muslim State appears to me to be the final destiny of the Muslims, at least of North-West India."}, {"Q": "What was Iqbal's view on the Ulama's role in the state?", "A": "Iqbal was against the Ulama assuming state roles or the establishment of a council of Ulama.", "S": "As a matter of principle Iqbal was against the Ulama assuming state roles or the establishment of a council of Ulama because that would separate the functionaries of religion from laymen and for Iqbal there cannot be a juxtaposition of the spiritual and the temporal."}, {"Q": "What was Iqbal's view on the importance of certain values in Islam?", "A": "Iqbal considered these values as crucial both to the survival and development of mankind, and believed they constituted the essence of Islam.", "S": "Ever since the conversion of Mohammad Iqbal, sometime during his stay in Europe, from territorial nationalism to Islam, he considered certain values of ethical orientation as crucial both to the survival and development of mankind, values which, in his view, constituted the essence of Islam."}, {"Q": "What was the concept of Umma in Madina according to the Prophet Muhammad?", "A": "It was a community that included Jews, tribals, and Muslims, and was not confined to the Muslim community alone.", "S": "It was not Muslim community but it was Madinese community, all were included Jews were included; tribals were included and Muslims were included."}, {"Q": "Who participated in the second and third sessions of the Round Table Conference?", "A": "Iqbal", "S": "At the primary session of the Round Table Conference, which began in London on November 12, 1930. Iqbal participated in the second and the third sessions of the Conference."}, {"Q": "How does Iqbal view Western democracy?", "A": "Iqbal views Western democracy as the 